# Treat tasks as atomic — prerequisite-first, enumerated, completed

Tasks must be treated as atomic units with three properties: (1) all prerequisite artefacts and conditions are confirmed in place before execution begins, (2) every intended target is explicitly enumerated up front and verified afterward, and (3) the task is driven all the way to verified completion without pausing unnecessarily mid-stream. Partial execution, assumed completion, or mid-task confirmation pauses all introduce the same failure mode: work that appears done but isn't.

## Procedures
- Before starting a task that depends on prior artefacts (retrieved skills, curated datasets, compiled outputs), confirm those artefacts are actually present and valid.
- At the start of any batch or multi-target task, enumerate all intended targets explicitly; at the end, verify each one was actually processed.
- Once the approach for a task is fully reasoned, execute it straight through to functional and visual completion — do not pause for re-confirmation at intermediate steps.

